.TH AENIGMA-CONFIG 8 "2026-08-12" "aenigma 5.0.0" "Aenigma Administration Scripts"
.SH NAME
aenigma-config \- set or clear a property in the Aenigma application
configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aenigma-config
.B \-p
.I PROPERTY
.B \-v
.I PROPERTY_VALUE
.br
.B aenigma-config
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aenigma-config
updates a single property in the Aenigma application's JSON
configuration file,
.IR /etc/aenigma/appsettings.json ,
using
.BR jq (1).
.PP
.I PROPERTY
is a dot-separated path identifying the property to set, e.g.
.I Logging.LogLevel.Default
addresses the nested
.B Default
key inside
.B LogLevel
inside
.BR Logging .
If any intermediate object in the path does not already exist,
.BR jq 's
.B setpath
creates it.
.PP
.I PROPERTY_VALUE
is inspected and written to the configuration file with an inferred
JSON type:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the literal string
.I null
is written as JSON
.BR null ;
.IP \(bu 2
the literal strings
.I true
or
.I false
are written as a JSON boolean;
.IP \(bu 2
a value matching an integer or decimal number (optionally negative)
is written as a JSON number;
.IP \(bu 2
anything else is written as a JSON string.
.RE
.PP
The configuration file is rewritten via a temporary file and
.BR mv (1)
so that
.I appsettings.json
is never left partially written. After the update, the file's owner
is reset to
.B aenigma:aenigma
and its permissions to
.BR 700 .
.PP
.B aenigma-config
must be run as root.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " PROPERTY"
Dot-separated path of the configuration property to set, e.g.
.IR OnionService " or " Logging.LogLevel.Default .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-v " PROPERTY_VALUE"
The value to assign to
.IR PROPERTY .
Its JSON type (string, number, boolean, or null) is inferred
automatically from its literal text, as described above. Required.
.TP
.B \-h
Display a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The property was updated successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, the script was not run as root, or
.I /etc/aenigma/appsettings.json
does not exist.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/aenigma/appsettings.json
The Aenigma application configuration file; read and rewritten by
this script. Owned by
.B aenigma:aenigma
with mode
.B 700
after any update.
.SH EXAMPLES
Set the application's onion service hostname:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-config \-p OnionService \e
    \-v "o33eowc56dw2os5qehojnqcdwdqmobmgj76y67b6b3xgc47oedmde4yd.onion"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Set a nested numeric property:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-config \-p Server.Port \-v 8080
.EE
.RE
.PP
Clear a property by setting it to JSON null:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo aenigma-config \-p FeatureFlags.Beta \-v null
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
There is no built-in validation that
.I PROPERTY
corresponds to a key the Aenigma application actually reads, nor
that
.I PROPERTY_VALUE
is well-formed for its intended use (e.g. a valid hostname, port
number, or enum value); the script only guarantees the write
produces syntactically valid JSON. Misconfigured properties will not
be caught until the application is restarted.
.PP
Because value type is inferred from literal text, a value that
should be a JSON string but happens to look like a number, boolean,
or the word
.I null
will be written using that inferred type instead. There is currently
no way to force a value to be written as a string if its text
matches one of these patterns.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR jq (1),
.BR aenigma-tor (8),
.BR aenigma-proxy (8)
